export const SCORING_UNIT = {"id":"u6","title":"Scoring Deep Dive","chapters":["ch14","ch15","ch16","ch17"]};
export const SCORING_CHAPTERS = [{"id":"ch14","unit":"u6","number":14,"title":"The Defect Impact Weight Table","subtitle":"The numbers, the structure behind them, and how to turn a punch list into a score projection","sections":[{"heading":"The table","body":"                    Unit      Inside    Outside\nLife-Threatening    60.0      54.5      49.6\nSevere              40.0      36.4      33.1\nModerate            20.0      18.2      16.5\nLow                  2.4       2.2       2.0\n\nTHE FORMULA THAT USES IT\nPoints deducted for a deficiency = (Defect Impact Weight) x (number of that deficiency) divided by (number of units in the inspected sample)\nScore = 100 minus the sum of all such deductions.\n\nConfirm this table against HUD's published NSPIRE scoring notice for the applicable inspection year before you put it in a client deliverable. HUD has revised scoring guidance more than once. Verifying the current values takes ten minutes and it is part of what a client is paying you for.\n\nTHE STRUCTURE — two ratios that make the table memorable\nRead down any column and the severity ladder is: LT, then Severe at about two-thirds of LT, then Moderate at exactly half of Severe, then Low at a small fraction.\nRead across any row and the area spread is remarkably tight: Inside is about 91 percent of Unit, and Outside is about 83 percent of Unit, at EVERY severity level. Check it: 54.5 divided by 60.0 is 0.908; 36.4 divided by 40.0 is 0.910; 18.2 divided by 20.0 is 0.910; 2.2 divided by 2.4 is 0.917. Outside: 0.827, 0.828, 0.825, 0.833.\n\nThat consistency is the useful part. Once you know a Unit weight you can reconstruct the other two closely enough for field triage: multiply by 0.91 for Inside and 0.83 for Outside.\n\nTHE THREE FACTS THIS TABLE ENCODES\n\nFACT 1 — SEVERITY DOMINATES LOCATION. In the Unit column, Life-Threatening is 25 times Low. Across any row, the widest area spread is about 17 percent. A consultant chasing the map instead of the ladder is optimizing the wrong variable by more than an order of magnitude.\n\nFACT 2 — SEVERE IS EXACTLY DOUBLE MODERATE. In every column, without exception. That makes field triage trivial: one Severe correction equals two Moderate corrections in points. If a Severe fix costs less than two Moderate fixes, do the Severe one.\n\nFACT 3 — THE MODERATE-TO-LOW CLIFF. Moderate is more than eight times Low. This is the boundary where careless classification does the most damage to a projection, and it is the boundary worth spending ten minutes on when a finding could plausibly be either.\n\nA NOTE ON PRECISION\nYour projection will not match the final report exactly, and you should never imply it will. You do not know the sample size in advance, you do not know which units will be drawn, and an inspector may find things you did not or decline to cite things you did. What the table gives you is a defensible, reproducible method and a RANKING that is robust even when the absolute number moves. Sell the ranking."},{"heading":"Single-deficiency calculations","body":"Do enough of these that the arithmetic becomes automatic. The whole model is one division.\n\nEXAMPLE 1 — one Life-Threatening unit deficiency at three property sizes\nSample of 8: 60.0 / 8 = 7.50 points\nSample of 16: 60.0 / 16 = 3.75 points\nSample of 32: 60.0 / 32 = 1.88 points\nSame condition, same 24-hour clock, four-fold spread in score impact.\n\nEXAMPLE 2 — the severity ladder at a fixed sample of 20, Unit area\nLife-Threatening: 60.0 / 20 = 3.00 points\nSevere: 40.0 / 20 = 2.00 points\nModerate: 20.0 / 20 = 1.00 point\nLow: 2.4 / 20 = 0.12 points\nRead that column and the strategy writes itself. Twenty-five Low corrections buy what one Life-Threatening correction buys.\n\nEXAMPLE 3 — the area ladder at a fixed sample of 20, Severe\nUnit: 40.0 / 20 = 2.00 points\nInside: 36.4 / 20 = 1.82 points\nOutside: 33.1 / 20 = 1.66 points\nThe total spread from Unit to Outside is 0.34 points — about 17 percent. Compare that to the severity spread in Example 2 and you can see why location is a second-order concern.\n\nEXAMPLE 4 — multiple instances of DIFFERENT standards\nThree different Severe deficiencies in units, sample of 12: 40.0 x 3 = 120.0, divided by 12 = 10.00 points.\nNote the word DIFFERENT. Three instances of the SAME standard within the Unit area collapse to one under the duplicate rule: 40.0 / 12 = 3.33 points.\nThe difference between those two numbers — 10.00 versus 3.33 — is the single largest source of error in amateur projections. Before you multiply, ask whether the instances are different standards or repeats of one.\n\nEXAMPLE 5 — a mixed handful at a sample of 25\n1 LT unit (60.0), 2 Severe unit different standards (80.0), 1 Severe inside (36.4), 4 Moderate unit different standards (80.0), 6 Low outside different standards (12.0).\nTotal weight: 60.0 + 80.0 + 36.4 + 80.0 + 12.0 = 268.4\nDeduction: 268.4 / 25 = 10.74 points\nScore: 89.26\nUnit-area weight: 60.0 + 80.0 + 80.0 = 220.0; Unit deduction 220.0 / 25 = 8.80 — well under 30, so the Unit Threshold is not triggered.\nNote the six Low outside findings contributed 0.48 points of the 10.74. That is 4 percent of the damage from what will feel like half the punch list."},{"heading":"Building a full projection from a punch list","body":"THE SEVEN-STEP METHOD. Follow it in this order every time; the order prevents the two most common mistakes.\n\nSTEP 1 — LIST EVERY FINDING WITH THREE ATTRIBUTES: standard name, inspectable area, severity. Not description — standard name. If you cannot name the standard, you cannot defend the classification.\n\nSTEP 2 — APPLY THE DUPLICATE RULE WITHIN EACH AREA. Group by standard within each of Unit, Inside and Outside, and reduce each group to one scored instance for that area. Keep the full instance count in a separate column, because the client needs it for the correction plan even though the score does not use it.\n\nSTEP 3 — SEPARATE THE DEFERRED-SCORING ITEMS. Fire-labeled doors, GFCI/AFCI, guardrails, HVAC, interior lighting, and minimum electrical and lighting do not deduct before October 1, 2026. Put them in their own block and produce two totals: the score as of today, and the score as it would be with those items scoring. The gap between those numbers is one of the most useful things you can hand a client right now.\n\nSTEP 4 — LOOK UP THE WEIGHT for each scored instance and total the weight WITHIN EACH AREA. Three subtotals: Unit weight, Inside weight, Outside weight.\n\nSTEP 5 — DIVIDE EACH AREA SUBTOTAL BY THE SAMPLE SIZE. Three area deductions.\n\nSTEP 6 — TEST THE UNIT THRESHOLD FIRST, before you compute the score. If the Unit deduction is 30 or more, the score is adjusted to 59 and the property fails on that basis. Do this test first so you never present a comfortable-looking projection that is actually a failure.\n\nSTEP 7 — SUM THE THREE AREA DEDUCTIONS AND SUBTRACT FROM 100.\n\nWORKED PROJECTION — a 100-unit property, sample of 20\nAfter de-duplication, the scored instances are:\nUNIT: 1 LT (60.0), 3 Severe (120.0), 5 Moderate (100.0), 2 Low (4.8). Subtotal 284.8. Deduction 284.8 / 20 = 14.24\nINSIDE: 1 Severe (36.4), 2 Moderate (36.4). Subtotal 72.8. Deduction 72.8 / 20 = 3.64\nOUTSIDE: 1 Moderate (16.5), 3 Low (6.0). Subtotal 22.5. Deduction 22.5 / 20 = 1.13\nUnit Threshold test: 14.24, well under 30. Not triggered.\nTotal deduction: 19.01. PROJECTED SCORE: 81.\n\nHOW TO PRESENT IT\nGive the client four numbers, in this order, and no more on the front page:\n1. Projected score, with a range. \"81, with a plausible range of 76 to 85 depending on sample composition.\"\n2. Unit Threshold status. \"Unit deductions project at 14.2 against a 30-point limit. Not at risk.\"\n3. Deferred-scoring exposure. \"An additional 4.1 points currently deferred, which begins deducting October 1, 2026.\"\n4. Points available from the top five corrections, with their cost.\nEverything else — the full line-item table, the photographs, the instance counts — goes behind those four numbers. A client who cannot find the answer on the first page will not read the rest."},{"heading":"The Unit Threshold, worked four ways","body":"THE RULE: if 30 OR MORE points are deducted from the UNIT inspectable area, the score is adjusted to 59 — a failure — regardless of the overall score. There is no equivalent threshold for Inside or Outside. Note the direction of the adjustment: it caps the score at 59. A property whose raw score is already below 59 does not improve; the raw score stands.\n\nCASE 1 — the clean trap\nA 30-unit property, sample of 14. Unit findings only: 2 LT (120.0), 6 Severe (240.0), 5 Moderate (100.0). Unit weight 460.0.\nUnit deduction: 460.0 / 14 = 32.86\nInside and Outside: clean.\nRaw score: 100 - 32.86 = 67.14. Looks like a comfortable pass.\nUnit Threshold: 32.86 is at or above 30. SCORE ADJUSTED TO 59. FAIL.\nThe client's reaction will be disbelief. Have the arithmetic ready.\n\nCASE 2 — the cheapest way out of Case 1\nClear one Life-Threatening unit finding: Unit weight drops to 400.0, deduction 400.0 / 14 = 28.57. Below 30 — threshold cleared, and the score becomes 71.43.\nONE REPAIR. That is the entire deliverable. A consultant who can say \"this specific item, and only this item, is the difference between a 59 and a 71\" has justified their fee for the year.\nClear one Severe as well and the deduction falls to 25.71 for a score of 74.29 — useful margin, because your projection is not exact and you want room between the projected Unit deduction and the 30-point line.\n\nCASE 3 — both thresholds failed\nA 48-unit property, sample of 16. Unit: 3 LT (180.0), 5 Severe (200.0), 6 Moderate (120.0) = 500.0, deduction 31.25. Inside: 1 Severe (36.4), deduction 2.28. Outside: 2 Moderate (33.0), deduction 2.06.\nTotal deduction 35.59, raw score 64.41 — which would pass. Unit deduction 31.25 is at or above 30. SCORE ADJUSTED TO 59. FAIL.\nThe remediation lesson: the Inside and Outside work here is worth 4.34 points combined and does NOTHING for the threshold. Every dollar must go into the Unit area until the Unit deduction is under 30 with margin.\n\nCASE 4 — the threshold that does not bind\nA 76-unit property, sample of 20. Unit deduction 17.60, Inside 13.40, Outside 9.26. Total 40.26, score 59.74 — a fail on the Property Threshold by a quarter of a point.\nUnit Threshold: 17.60, nowhere near 30. Not triggered.\nHere the strategy is the opposite of Case 3: points can be harvested anywhere, and the cheapest points are in Inside and Outside. Clearing one Inside LT, three Inside Severe and two Outside Severe items takes the score from 59.74 to 71.24 without entering a single dwelling unit.\n\nTHE RULE OF THUMB TO CARRY\nRun the Unit Threshold test first, every time. Then ask which regime you are in:\n• Unit deduction at or near 30 — all money goes to Unit-area findings, in severity order, until you are under 30 with several points of margin.\n• Unit deduction comfortably under 30 — money goes wherever points are cheapest, which is usually Inside first.\nThose are genuinely different remediation plans, and choosing the wrong one wastes the client's entire budget."},{"heading":"Sample size — what you can and cannot know","body":"HOW THE SAMPLE IS DRAWN\nHUD sizes the scoring sample to reach a 90 PERCENT CONFIDENCE LEVEL with a 6 PERCENT MARGIN OF ERROR. The sample grows with property size but far more slowly, and it is capped: the maximum number of units in the scoring sample under NSPIRE is 32, raised from 27 under UPCS. Small properties can have all or nearly all units inspected.\n\nWHAT NOT TO DO\nDo not reproduce a sample-size-by-property-size table from a secondary source and present it as authoritative. Sample size is determined by HUD's methodology for the specific inspection, and the number that matters is the one printed on the property's own report.\n\nWHAT TO DO INSTEAD\n1. ASK FOR THE PRIOR REPORT and read the sample size off it. This is the single best input you have.\n2. IF THERE IS NO PRIOR REPORT, run the projection at two or three plausible sample sizes and present a RANGE with the assumption stated. \"At a sample of 16 the projection is 78; at 24 it is 85. We have assumed 20.\"\n3. SAY WHICH NUMBER YOU USED, in writing, on the front page. A projection without a stated sample assumption is not a projection, it is a number.\n\nWHY THE DIVISOR CUTS BOTH WAYS\nA larger sample divides every deduction by a larger number, which reduces the impact of each finding — but it also means more units are examined, so more findings are likely to be recorded. Those two effects partly offset. What does not offset is the systemic condition: a deficiency present in most units appears in the sample regardless of its size, and its deduction shrinks as the sample grows.\n\nWORKED COMPARISON — isolated versus systemic\nProperty A: 150 units, three genuinely bad units, everything else fine. Sample of 32.\nProperty B: 150 units, all units in fair condition, but no GFCI protection anywhere and no bathroom ventilation in one 40-unit floor plan. Sample of 32.\nProperty A's exposure is probabilistic — the sample may draw none, one, or all three bad units, and the projection should be given as a range reflecting that.\nProperty B's exposure is deterministic — both conditions will be found, and both will be scored once each in the Unit area.\nCounterintuitively, Property B is easier to project and Property A is harder, which is the opposite of how they feel on a walk-through.\n\nWHAT TO TELL A CLIENT ABOUT THE SAMPLE, IN ONE SENTENCE\n\"You cannot prepare the sample, you can only prepare the property — and the conditions that actually decide your score are the ones that repeat, because those are present wherever the sample lands.\""},{"heading":"Ranking a punch list by points recovered per dollar","body":"This is the deliverable clients cannot produce internally, and it is where the fee is.\n\nTHE METHOD\nFor every scored instance:\n1. Points recovered = (weight) divided by (sample size). Remember that clearing a repeated deficiency recovers points only if you eliminate it ENTIRELY from that area — partial remediation of a repeat recovers nothing.\n2. Cost = the full cost to eliminate the condition from the area, including every instance, not just one.\n3. Ratio = points per thousand dollars.\n4. Sort descending. Then override the sort for two things: anything on a 24-hour clock goes first regardless of ratio, and anything needed to clear the Unit Threshold goes next.\n\nWORKED RANKING — a 100-unit property, sample of 20\nFinding A: 1 LT exposed conductor, missing panel deadfront, Unit area. 60.0 / 20 = 3.00 points. Cost about 40 dollars. Ratio: 75 points per thousand dollars.\nFinding B: 1 Severe broken entry-door deadbolt, Unit area, present in 3 units. 40.0 / 20 = 2.00 points. Cost about 150 dollars for all three. Ratio: 13.3 per thousand.\nFinding C: 1 Severe mold-like substance 4 square feet, Unit area, driven by a bathroom fan failure in 6 units. 40.0 / 20 = 2.00 points. Cost about 4,200 dollars for fans plus remediation. Ratio: 0.5 per thousand.\nFinding D: 1 Severe sprinkler obstruction, Inside area, one storage room. 36.4 / 20 = 1.82 points. Cost: an afternoon of labor, call it 200 dollars. Ratio: 9.1 per thousand.\nFinding E: 3 Low outside items. 6.0 / 20 = 0.30 points. Cost about 600 dollars. Ratio: 0.5 per thousand.\n\nSorted: A (75), B (13.3), D (9.1), C (0.5), E (0.5).\nThen apply the overrides: A is Life-Threatening and on a 24-hour clock, so it was going first anyway. Nothing here is needed for the Unit Threshold.\nRecommended sequence: A, B, D, then C, and E only if budget remains.\nTotal from A, B and D: 6.82 points for roughly 390 dollars.\nTotal from C and E: 2.30 points for roughly 4,800 dollars.\n\nTHE SENTENCE THAT LANDS\n\"The first 390 dollars buys you 6.8 points. The next 4,800 buys you 2.3. Both are worth doing, but do them in that order, and if the budget stops, stop after the first group.\"\n\nTHREE HONEST CAVEATS TO INCLUDE EVERY TIME\n1. Costs are estimates, and the ranking is more reliable than the absolute numbers.\n2. Items in the deferred-scoring group recover ZERO points before October 1, 2026 — show them in a separate block with their post-date value so the client can plan rather than be surprised.\n3. Safety obligations do not follow the ranking. A Life-Threatening item is corrected within 24 hours whether or not it tops the ratio list, and every instance of a repeated deficiency is corrected whether or not the score notices."}]},{"id":"ch15","unit":"u6","number":15,"title":"Using the NSPIRE Scoring Calculator","subtitle":"What HUD's calculator does, how to build your own, and how to run the what-if scenarios that clients actually pay for","sections":[{"heading":"What the calculator is and what it is not","body":"HUD publishes an NSPIRE Score Calculator alongside the standards and the inspection protocol. It implements the scoring model from Chapter 14: you supply the deficiencies by severity and inspectable area and the unit sample size, and it returns the score.\n\nWHAT IT DOES\n• Applies the Defect Impact Weights.\n• Divides by the unit sample.\n• Subtracts from 100.\n• Applies the threshold logic.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT DO, AND WHY THIS MATTERS TO YOUR PRACTICE\n• IT DOES NOT DECIDE SEVERITY. You supply that. Every classification error you make propagates straight through the arithmetic. The calculator is precise, not accurate — it will confidently compute a wrong answer from wrong inputs.\n• IT DOES NOT APPLY THE DUPLICATE RULE FOR YOU. If you enter five instances of the same standard within the same area, the arithmetic will treat them as five. You must de-duplicate before entry.\n• IT DOES NOT KNOW YOUR SAMPLE SIZE. You supply it, and it is the single most influential number in the whole calculation.\n• IT DOES NOT KNOW ABOUT THE DEFERRED-SCORING ITEMS unless you exclude them yourself. Fire-labeled doors, GFCI/AFCI, guardrails, HVAC, interior lighting, and minimum electrical and lighting do not deduct before October 1, 2026.\n• IT DOES NOT PRODUCE A REMEDIATION PLAN. It gives you a number. The ranking, the sequencing and the cost analysis are yours, and they are what the client is actually buying.\n\nTHE PROFESSIONAL POSTURE\nUse HUD's calculator to CHECK your work, not to do it. Build your own model as well, because you need to run what-if scenarios rapidly in front of a client and because you need the intermediate numbers — the area subtotals, the Unit Threshold test, the deferred-item block — that a single-answer tool does not surface.\n\nAnd verify the calculator's version against the scoring guidance for the applicable inspection year. A calculator built on a superseded weight table produces confident, wrong numbers, which is worse than no calculator at all."},{"heading":"Building your own model in a spreadsheet","body":"Twenty minutes of setup that you will use on every engagement for years. Any spreadsheet application will do.\n\nSHEET 1 — WEIGHTS\nA four-by-three lookup table exactly as published:\n                    Unit      Inside    Outside\nLife-Threatening    60.0      54.5      49.6\nSevere              40.0      36.4      33.1\nModerate            20.0      18.2      16.5\nLow                  2.4       2.2       2.0\nPut the source and the version date in a cell next to it. Every time you open the file you should see how current it is.\n\nSHEET 2 — FINDINGS\nOne row per observed finding, with these columns:\nA. Standard name\nB. Area (Unit / Inside / Outside)\nC. Severity (LT / Severe / Moderate / Low)\nD. Location (building, unit, room)\nE. Measurement recorded\nF. Instance count at this location\nG. Deferred-scoring flag (yes / no)\nH. Photo reference\nI. Estimated cost to eliminate the condition entirely from the area\nJ. Correction clock (24 hours / 30 days / 60 days), read off the standard\n\nSHEET 3 — SCORED INSTANCES\nBuilt from Sheet 2 by grouping on standard name plus area, so each combination appears exactly once. This is where the duplicate rule is applied, and doing it as a separate sheet rather than in your head is what stops you from getting it wrong.\nColumns: standard, area, severity, weight looked up from Sheet 1, deferred flag, total instance count carried over, total cost carried over.\n\nSHEET 4 — CALCULATION\n• Sample size, entered once in a single clearly labelled cell. Every formula references it. This is what lets you re-run the whole projection at a different sample size by changing one number.\n• Unit weight subtotal, excluding deferred items.\n• Inside weight subtotal, excluding deferred items.\n• Outside weight subtotal, excluding deferred items.\n• Three area deductions: each subtotal divided by the sample size.\n• UNIT THRESHOLD TEST: a cell that reads FAIL when the Unit deduction is 30 or more.\n• Raw score: 100 minus the sum of the three deductions.\n• Final score: 59 if the Unit Threshold is triggered and the raw score is above 59, otherwise the raw score.\n• A parallel block computing the same figures INCLUDING the deferred items, so you always have both numbers.\n\nSHEET 5 — RANKING\nOne row per scored instance: points recovered (weight divided by sample size), cost, points per thousand dollars, correction clock, and a manual priority override column for 24-hour items and Unit Threshold clearers.\n\nTHE TEST THAT PROVES YOUR MODEL WORKS\nRebuild worked example 5 from Chapter 4: a sample of 20 with Unit weight 284.8, Inside 72.8, Outside 22.5. Your model should return deductions of 14.24, 3.64 and 1.13, a total of 19.01, and a score of 81. If it does not, the error is almost always in the de-duplication step on Sheet 3 or a mis-anchored sample-size reference on Sheet 4."},{"heading":"Entering findings without corrupting the result","body":"Five disciplines. Each one exists because of a specific, common failure.\n\nDISCIPLINE 1 — ENTER STANDARD NAMES, NOT DESCRIPTIONS\n\"Loose railing in stairwell B\" cannot be grouped, cannot be de-duplicated, and cannot be defended in a technical review. \"Guardrail — undersized, 27 inches, Inside, stairwell B\" can be all three. If you cannot name the standard, you have not finished the observation.\n\nDISCIPLINE 2 — DE-DUPLICATE ON THE SHEET, NOT IN YOUR HEAD\nThe grouping is by STANDARD plus AREA. Two traps:\n• Collapsing across areas. The same standard in a unit and in a corridor is TWO scored instances at different weights, and only the Unit one feeds the threshold.\n• Collapsing different standards that feel similar. A missing outlet cover and a missing GFCI are different standards and both score.\n\nDISCIPLINE 3 — RECORD THE MEASUREMENT IN ITS OWN COLUMN\nNot in the description. A separate column, with the number. It is what lets you sort for borderline findings, and it is what you produce in a technical review. \"Mold-like substance, bathroom ceiling, 3.5 sq ft cumulative\" is defensible; \"significant mold\" is not.\n\nDISCIPLINE 4 — FLAG THE DEFERRED ITEMS AT ENTRY\nNot later. The six categories are easy to spot at the moment of observation and easy to forget at the moment of calculation. A projection that silently includes deferred items overstates the client's current exposure and understates their future one — wrong in both directions at once.\n\nDISCIPLINE 5 — COST THE FULL ELIMINATION, NOT THE SINGLE INSTANCE\nFor any repeated condition, the cost column must be what it takes to remove the condition from the entire area, because that is what it takes to recover the points. A missing GFCI that appears in 71 of 96 units costs 71 receptacles and the labor, not one.\n\nWORKED ENTRY — six raw observations reduced to scored instances\nRaw observations:\n1. Missing outlet cover, unit 104. 2. Missing outlet cover, unit 211. 3. Missing outlet cover, corridor 2. 4. Missing GFCI within 6 feet of sink, unit 104. 5. Missing GFCI within 6 feet of sink, unit 318. 6. Guardrail 27 inches at exterior walkway.\nScored instances after de-duplication:\n• Electrical — Conductor, Outlet, and Switch, UNIT area, one instance. Instance count 2.\n• Electrical — Conductor, Outlet, and Switch, INSIDE area, one instance. Instance count 1.\n• Electrical — GFCI, UNIT area, one instance. Instance count 2. DEFERRED FLAG YES.\n• Guardrail, OUTSIDE area, one instance. Instance count 1. DEFERRED FLAG YES.\nSix observations, four scored instances, two of which do not deduct before October 1, 2026. Getting this reduction right is most of the skill in using any calculator."},{"heading":"Running what-if scenarios","body":"This is what the model is for. A static projection is worth something; the ability to answer a client's questions in real time is worth considerably more.\n\nTHE FIVE QUESTIONS CLIENTS ASK, AND HOW TO ANSWER THEM\n\nQUESTION 1 — \"What if we only have 5,000 dollars?\"\nSort Sheet 5 by points per thousand dollars, apply the two overrides, and take items until the budget is exhausted. Report the resulting score and the Unit Threshold status.\nWorked: at a 100-unit property with a sample of 20 projecting 81, the top three items by ratio recover 6.82 points for roughly 390 dollars, taking the projection to about 88. The next 4,800 dollars recovers 2.30 points, reaching about 90. So the honest answer is: 390 dollars gets you to 88, and reaching 90 costs another 4,800.\n\nQUESTION 2 — \"What do we need to hit 90 and get on the three-year cycle?\"\nCompute the required deduction reduction, convert to weight by multiplying by the sample size, then select items from the ranking until the weight target is met.\nWorked: a 60-unit property, sample of 20, projecting 81.32. To reach 90 you need to shed 8.68 points, which at a sample of 20 is 173.6 of weight. Clearing 2 Moderate unit instances (40.0), 1 Severe inside (36.4), 1 Moderate inside (18.2), 2 Moderate outside (33.0) and a handful of Low items gets you there — and the model tells you the exact combination rather than making you guess.\n\nQUESTION 3 — \"What happens on October 1, 2026?\"\nRun the parallel block that includes the deferred items and report both numbers.\nWorked: a property projecting 84 today with GFCI, guardrail and interior lighting conditions carrying 4.1 points of deferred weight projects at about 80 once those items begin scoring. That single sentence — \"you are an 84 today and a 79.9 on October 1 unless these six items are closed\" — is the most persuasive thing you can currently say to a scored-program owner.\n\nQUESTION 4 — \"What if the sample is bigger or smaller than you assumed?\"\nChange one cell. Report the range.\nWorked: the same punch list at samples of 16, 20 and 24 produces projections of 76.3, 81.0 and 84.2. Give the client all three and say which you consider most likely and why.\n\nQUESTION 5 — \"What is our worst case?\"\nAdd the deficiencies you believe exist in unsampled units and re-run. This is a judgment exercise, and you should label it as one — but a client facing a borderline result deserves to see the downside, and showing it is what makes your central estimate credible.\n\nTHE DISCIPLINE THAT KEEPS SCENARIOS HONEST\nSave each scenario as a labelled column, not as an edit to the base case. You want to be able to show the client the base case and every variant side by side. A model that has been edited into a single optimistic answer is worse than no model, and you will not remember which assumptions you changed."},{"heading":"Limitations, honest framing, and the report language","body":"WHERE THE MODEL IS WEAKEST — say all of these out loud\n1. SAMPLE COMPOSITION. You do not know which units will be drawn. Any punch list built on a walk of some units is an inference about the rest.\n2. YOUR SEVERITY CALLS. Precise arithmetic on a misclassified finding produces a precise wrong answer, and the Moderate-to-Low boundary is where the damage is largest.\n3. INSPECTOR JUDGMENT. Two competent inspectors will not produce identical reports. Some of your findings will not be cited; some conditions you passed over will be.\n4. TIMING. Conditions change between your walk and the inspection — seasonally, through resident behavior, and through the repairs themselves.\n5. STANDARD AND GUIDANCE REVISIONS. HUD versions the standards and has revised scoring guidance. A model built on last year's table is confidently wrong.\n\nHOW TO PRESENT UNCERTAINTY WITHOUT UNDERMINING YOURSELF\nThe instinct is to hedge everything, which makes the report useless. The better approach is to be precise about the METHOD and honest about the INPUTS:\n\n\"Projected score: 81, with a plausible range of 76 to 85.\nAssumptions: unit sample of 20, taken from the prior inspection report dated [date]. Deficiencies as observed on [date] across [n] units, [n] common areas and the full site.\nExcluded from the projection: six affirmative requirement categories that do not deduct before October 1, 2026. Including them, the same conditions project at 76.9.\nUnit Threshold: Unit-area deductions project at 14.2 against a 30-point limit. Not at risk.\nThis projection is not a guarantee of the inspection result. It applies HUD's published scoring model to the conditions observed. Sample composition and inspector judgment will produce variation.\"\n\nTHE PARAGRAPH TO PUT IN EVERY REPORT\n\"Score projections in this report apply the Defect Impact Weight table and scoring formula published by HUD as of [version date], to the deficiencies recorded during our inspection, at the stated unit sample size. Severity classifications reflect the NSPIRE standards as published at that date. Where a standard could not be verified against current published guidance, it is identified as unverified in the line item. This is a projection, not a prediction of the official result.\"\n\nWHAT THAT PARAGRAPH BUYS YOU\nIt states the method, dates the standards, discloses the assumptions, flags what you could not verify, and declines to promise an outcome. Every one of those is protective — and, more usefully, every one of them signals a level of rigor that most competitors will not match. Clients notice.\n\nA FINAL WORD ON THE UNVERIFIED-ITEM CONVENTION\nWhen you cannot confirm a specific criterion or weight against current published guidance, say so in the line item rather than omitting it or guessing. A report that reads \"Standard: [name]. Observed: [measurement]. Severity per current standard to be confirmed\" is professional. A report that quietly guesses is a liability, and one that silently drops the finding is worse. Clients tolerate acknowledged uncertainty; they do not forgive discovered fabrication."}]},{"id":"ch16","unit":"u6","number":16,"title":"Reading a REAC Inspection Report","subtitle":"What the report contains, the order to read it in, and how to reverse-engineer a score from it","sections":[{"heading":"What the report contains","body":"For a scored inspection, HUD issues a DRAFT inspection report with a draft score and the full record of defects, and then a FINAL inspection report with a final score after the technical review process. Both provide summaries of the inspection results.\n\nWHAT IS IN THE REPORT\n• THE SCORES.\n• BUILDING AND UNIT INSPECTION DATA.\n• OCCUPANCY INFORMATION.\n• A DEFICIENCY SUMMARY.\n• A BREAKDOWN OF THE UNITS SAMPLED.\n• IMAGES OF ANY CERTIFICATES provided to the inspector.\n• DETAIL ON LOW, MODERATE, SEVERE AND LIFE-THREATENING DEFICIENCIES.\n• THE HEALTH AND SAFETY REPORT.\n\nReport formats change. Rather than memorizing a layout that may be superseded, learn to find the SEVEN THINGS below in whatever format you are handed. If you can locate all seven, you can work any version of the report.\n\nTHE SEVEN THINGS TO FIND, IN THIS ORDER\n\n1. THE SAMPLE SIZE — the number of units inspected. Everything else is arithmetic on this number, and it is the input you most need for future projections. Find it in the units-sampled breakdown.\n2. THE SCORE, and whether the report is DRAFT or FINAL. Draft means the technical review window is live; final means it has closed. Check which you are holding before you promise a client anything.\n3. THE DATE THE REPORT WAS PROVIDED. The technical review deadline runs from it — under the NSPIRE provisions for Multifamily, no later than the 45th CALENDAR day following the day HUD provides the report.\n4. THE AREA BREAKDOWN — how much deduction came from Unit, Inside and Outside. This is what tells you whether the Unit Threshold was in play and where the remediation money belongs.\n5. THE DEFICIENCY DETAIL — every recorded deficiency with its standard, location and severity. This is the working document.\n6. THE HEALTH AND SAFETY REPORT — the health and safety deficiencies driving correction obligations. Read it separately from the score. A good score does not discharge a single correction obligation.\n7. THE CERTIFICATE IMAGES — which certificates were provided and which were not. Useful both for the client's own file and for spotting a citation that should not have been recorded.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST WHEN A CLIENT HANDS YOU A REPORT\nCalculate the technical review deadline date and put it in writing at the top of your engagement letter. More technical reviews are lost to the calendar than to the merits, and it takes thirty seconds."},{"heading":"Reading the score and the area breakdown","body":"THE THREE QUESTIONS THE SCORE ANSWERS\n1. PASS OR FAIL. Sixty and above passes on the Property Threshold.\n2. WHICH INSPECTION CYCLE. Ninety and above puts the property on at least a three-year cycle; 80 to 89 is a two-year cycle; below 80 is annual.\n3. WAS THE UNIT THRESHOLD IN PLAY. Thirty or more points of Unit-area deduction adjusts the score to 59.\n\nA SCORE OF EXACTLY 59 DESERVES A SECOND LOOK. It may be a genuine 59, or it may be a score that was ADJUSTED to 59 by the Unit Threshold from a higher raw figure. Those are different situations with different remediation plans, and the area breakdown tells you which one you have. If the Unit deduction is at or above 30, you are looking at an adjusted score, and every dollar belongs in the Unit area until that number is comfortably below 30.\n\nREADING THE AREA BREAKDOWN — THE FOUR PATTERNS\nTake the three area deductions and see which shape you have. Each implies a different plan.\n\nPATTERN 1 — UNIT-DOMINATED. Unit deduction is most of the total.\nMeaning: in-unit conditions, possibly systemic across a floor plan. Check the Unit Threshold immediately. Remediation is expensive because it means entering units, scheduling residents, and often repeating work across many units.\n\nPATTERN 2 — INSIDE-DOMINATED. Inside deduction is a large share.\nMeaning: common areas and building systems. This is the best news a client can get, because Inside conditions are cheap, need no resident coordination, and are entirely under management's control. Chapter 4's worked example 6 is this pattern — a property that failed at 52.2 and could reach 71.1 without entering a single unit.\n\nPATTERN 3 — EVENLY SPREAD. All three areas contribute meaningfully.\nMeaning: a general maintenance deficit rather than a specific failure. Remediation is a program, not a project, and the honest conversation is about staffing and process rather than a punch list.\n\nPATTERN 4 — ONE ENORMOUS FINDING. A single Life-Threatening item dominates.\nMeaning: at a small property, one LT finding at a sample of 8 is 7.50 points on its own. Verify the classification carefully, because a single item carrying that much weight is also the highest-value technical review target if it was misclassified.\n\nWORKED READ — an area breakdown\nA report shows Unit deductions 31.25, Inside 2.28, Outside 2.06, and a score of 59.\nRead: total deduction 35.59, so the raw score was 64.41. The score of 59 is an ADJUSTED score — the Unit deduction of 31.25 crossed the threshold. Pattern 1.\nWhat this tells the client: the Inside and Outside work on this property is worth 4.34 points combined and does nothing for the threshold. Every dollar goes into Unit-area findings, in severity order, until the Unit deduction is under 30 with margin. A plan that starts with the parking lot fails again."},{"heading":"Working the deficiency detail","body":"The deficiency detail is where your billable work happens. Read every line with four questions in mind.\n\nQUESTION 1 — DOES THIS CONDITION MEET THE STANDARD'S WRITTEN CRITERIA?\nThis is the criteria-not-met check and it is your best source of technical review grounds. Run it hardest against the standards that have numbers:\n• A trip hazard recorded where the vertical differential is under 3/4 inch or the horizontal separation is under 2 inches.\n• A sprinkler obstruction recorded where the obstruction is a permanent built-environment feature such as an exit sign, wall cabinet or permanent light fixture, or a parked vehicle.\n• A fire extinguisher tag deficiency where a servicing company invoice or report dated within the past year exists.\n• An elevator certificate recorded as a deficiency at all, when the standard provides that neither its absence nor its expiration is itself a recorded deficiency.\n• A mold-like substance finding below the 4-square-inch de minimis floor.\n• An exposed conductor recorded at an opening measuring 1/2 inch or less with no conductor exposed.\n\nQUESTION 2 — IS THE SEVERITY RIGHT?\nCheck every escalating standard against its measurement. Mold at 4 to 9 square feet is Severe, not Life-Threatening. A single-unit pest sighting is not the multiple-units-and-common-areas escalation. This is where measured, dated photographs from your pre-inspection file earn their keep.\n\nQUESTION 3 — IS THE AREA RIGHT?\nA deficiency attributed to the Unit area that was observed in a common space, or attributed to a unit not on the sample list, is an error worth challenging — and because of the Unit Threshold it can matter far more than its weight suggests. Cross-check every Unit-area line against the units-sampled breakdown.\n\nQUESTION 4 — IS THIS A DEFERRED-SCORING ITEM?\nFire-labeled doors, GFCI/AFCI, guardrails, HVAC, interior lighting, and minimum electrical and lighting are cited but do not deduct before October 1, 2026. If one appears to have been scored before that date, that is a filing.\n\nTHEN GROUP THE DETAIL THREE WAYS\n• BY STANDARD, to find the systemic conditions. Any standard appearing repeatedly is a floor-plan, building or construction-era problem, and it is where the durable fix is.\n• BY UNIT, to find the units in genuinely poor condition, which usually indicates a turnover or maintenance-response problem rather than a building problem.\n• BY SEVERITY AND CLOCK, to build the correction schedule — 24-hour items first, then 30-day, then 60-day.\n\nWORKED READ — spotting the systemic condition\nA report lists 31 deficiencies across 16 sampled units. Grouping by standard shows: bathroom ventilation absent in 9 units; missing GFCI within 6 feet of water in 11 units; 11 assorted single-instance items.\nRead: two systemic conditions and a tail. Both systemic conditions score once each in the Unit area under the duplicate rule, so their SCORE contribution is modest — but they represent 20 of the 31 recorded deficiencies and the full correction cost, and one of them (GFCI) is in the deferred-scoring group and begins deducting October 1, 2026.\nWhat to tell the client: your score problem and your cost problem are different problems. The score came mostly from the eleven single items; the money is in the two systemic conditions; and one of the two gets worse on a known date."},{"heading":"Reverse-engineering a score","body":"Reconstructing the score from the deficiency detail is the best way to verify that the report is internally consistent, and it occasionally finds an arithmetic error worth filing on.\n\nTHE METHOD\n1. Read the sample size off the units-sampled breakdown.\n2. List every recorded deficiency with its standard, area and severity.\n3. De-duplicate by standard within each area.\n4. Look up the weight for each scored instance.\n5. Total by area, divide each by the sample size.\n6. Compare your three area deductions to the report's.\n7. Compare your total to the reported score.\n\nWORKED RECONSTRUCTION\nReport facts: 220-unit property, sample of 32, reported score 59.49.\nDeficiency detail, after de-duplication by standard within area:\nUNIT: 2 LT (120.0), 6 Severe (240.0), 14 Moderate (280.0), 9 Low (21.6). Subtotal 661.6. Deduction 661.6 / 32 = 20.68\nINSIDE: 1 LT (54.5), 4 Severe (145.6), 9 Moderate (163.8), 7 Low (15.4). Subtotal 379.3. Deduction 379.3 / 32 = 11.85\nOUTSIDE: 3 Severe (99.3), 8 Moderate (132.0), 12 Low (24.0). Subtotal 255.3. Deduction 255.3 / 32 = 7.98\nTotal deduction: 40.51. Score: 59.49. Matches the report.\nUnit Threshold: 20.68, under 30. Not triggered — so this is a genuine Property Threshold failure, not an adjusted 59.\n\nNOW USE THE RECONSTRUCTION\nThe property needs 0.51 points to reach 60. At a sample of 32, that is 16.3 of weight — LESS THAN ONE MODERATE OUTSIDE DEFICIENCY, which is 16.5.\nSo the single most valuable question is: is there ONE recorded deficiency anywhere on this report that does not meet its standard's written criteria? One successful criteria-not-met filing on any Moderate item anywhere on the property converts a failure into a pass.\nThat is what reverse-engineering buys you. Without the reconstruction you would be building a remediation plan; with it, you know the property is a quarter of a Severe finding away from passing and that the technical review is the highest-value action available.\n\nA SECOND USE — checking your own accuracy\nReconstruct the score on every report you are given, including ones where nothing is in dispute. Comparing your reconstruction to the reported figure calibrates your severity and de-duplication judgment against HUD's actual application, and it is the fastest way to get good at projections. If your reconstruction is consistently off in one direction, you have found a systematic error in your own classification habits.\n\nWHAT TO DO IF YOUR RECONSTRUCTION DOES NOT MATCH\nDo not assume the report is wrong. In order of likelihood: your de-duplication differs from HUD's; you have a severity wrong; you have an area attribution wrong; you used the wrong sample size; the weight table you used is not the one applicable to that inspection year. Work through them in that order. Only after all five are excluded is an arithmetic error on the report a plausible explanation — and if you get there, it is a strong filing."},{"heading":"Turning a report into an engagement","body":"A client handing you a report is a client with a problem and a deadline. Here is the sequence that turns it into work.\n\nSTEP 1 — THE DEADLINE, IN WRITING, FIRST\nCalculate the technical review deadline from the date HUD provided the report and put it at the top of everything you send. Thirty seconds, and it is the single most valuable thing you do in the first hour.\n\nSTEP 2 — THE FIVE-NUMBER SUMMARY\nBefore any analysis, give the client five numbers on one page:\n1. The score and whether it passed.\n2. Whether the score was adjusted by the Unit Threshold.\n3. The three area deductions.\n4. The number of 24-hour items and whether they have been corrected and documented.\n5. The date the technical review window closes.\nMost clients have never seen their own report reduced to five numbers, and doing it establishes immediately that you read it properly.\n\nSTEP 3 — THE TECHNICAL REVIEW SCAN\nRun the four questions from earlier in this chapter across every line. Produce a short list of candidate filings with the ground, the evidence needed, and the point effect of each. Then tell the client honestly whether it is worth filing. Sometimes it is not, and saying so is what makes you credible the time you say it is.\n\nSTEP 4 — THE CORRECTION SCHEDULE\nSorted by clock, not by cost: 24-hour items first, then 30-day, then 60-day. Cross-check against what has already been corrected and whether it was documented. Undocumented corrections are a recurring problem and a fixable one.\n\nSTEP 5 — THE FORWARD PLAN\nThis is where the engagement becomes ongoing rather than a one-off:\n• The systemic conditions from the grouping exercise, with costs and a sequence.\n• The deferred-scoring exposure and what the score becomes on October 1, 2026.\n• The next inspection window implied by the score's cycle.\n• The annual self-inspection obligation and whether the client can currently produce three years of records.\n• The certificate and service file, and what is missing from it.\n\nTHE SENTENCE THAT CONVERTS THE ENGAGEMENT\n\"This report tells you what happened. What I can tell you is what your next one will say, and what it will cost to change that.\"\n\nTwo cautions to keep the relationship honest:\n• Do not oversell the technical review. Most reports contain one or two defensible challenges, not ten. A consultant who finds ten grounds on every report is not reading carefully; they are selling.\n• Do not let the score become the only conversation. The Health and Safety Report and the correction obligations are independent of the number, and a client who has been focused on their score for three weeks may have an undocumented 24-hour correction sitting in their file. Ask."}]},{"id":"ch17","unit":"u6","number":17,"title":"Scored Practice Scenarios","subtitle":"Six full properties, worked end to end — classification, projection, threshold test, and the remediation plan","sections":[{"heading":"How to use this chapter","body":"Six properties. For each you get the raw findings, and you should work the projection yourself before reading the answer.\n\nTHE PROCEDURE, EVERY TIME\n1. Classify each finding by STANDARD, AREA and SEVERITY.\n2. De-duplicate by standard within each area.\n3. Separate the deferred-scoring items — fire-labeled doors, GFCI/AFCI, guardrails, HVAC, interior lighting, minimum electrical and lighting — into their own block.\n4. Total the weight by area.\n5. Divide each area subtotal by the sample size.\n6. TEST THE UNIT THRESHOLD FIRST. Thirty or more points of Unit deduction adjusts the score to 59.\n7. Sum the three deductions and subtract from 100.\n8. Build the remediation plan, ranked by points per dollar, with 24-hour items and threshold-clearers promoted to the top.\n\nTHE TABLE, so you do not have to turn back\n                    Unit      Inside    Outside\nLife-Threatening    60.0      54.5      49.6\nSevere              40.0      36.4      33.1\nModerate            20.0      18.2      16.5\nLow                  2.4       2.2       2.0\n\nWHAT THE SCENARIOS ARE DESIGNED TO TEACH\nScenario 1 — the small property with no statistical cushion.\nScenario 2 — the Unit Threshold trap, and the one repair that clears it.\nScenario 3 — the large property sitting a fraction of a point below passing.\nScenario 4 — the failing property, and building the recovery in the right order.\nScenario 5 — both thresholds failed at once, and why that changes the plan.\nScenario 6 — the cycle-boundary property, where the argument is money rather than compliance.\n\nAll figures below are projections built on the published weight table and a stated sample size. Confirm the current table and the property's actual sample size before using this method on a real engagement."},{"heading":"Scenario 1 — the small property with no cushion","body":"THE PROPERTY: 8-unit Public Housing site. Prior report shows a sample of 8 — all units inspected.\n\nRAW FINDINGS\n• Unit 3: exposed conductor, missing switch cover, 2-by-3-inch opening with conductors visible.\n• Units 3 and 6: entry door deadbolt broken, cannot be locked.\n• Unit 5: mold-like substance on bathroom ceiling, measured 3.5 square feet cumulative.\n• Units 1, 4 and 7: bathroom with no fan, no passive vent, and a window painted shut.\n• Unit 2: range with one inoperable burner.\n• Unit 8: kitchen sink supply leak with cabinet floor swollen.\n• Units 2 and 5: two different minor habitability items each meeting Low criteria.\n• Laundry room: stored boxes within 10 inches of a sprinkler head.\n• Corridor: damaged flooring creating a hazard.\n• Site: 1-inch sidewalk lift; deteriorated walkway surface.\n• Site: three separate Low items.\n\nCLASSIFY AND DE-DUPLICATE\nUNIT AREA\n• Exposed conductor — LT — 60.0\n• Entry door security (2 instances, one standard) — Severe — 40.0\n• Mold-like substance, 3.5 sq ft, in the 1-to-9 band — Severe — 40.0\n• Bathroom ventilation (3 instances, one standard) — Moderate — 20.0\n• Cooking appliance partially inoperable — Moderate — 20.0\n• Plumbing leak — Moderate — 20.0\n• Two Low standards — 2.4 x 2 = 4.8\nUnit subtotal: 204.8\nINSIDE AREA\n• Sprinkler obstruction — Severe — 36.4\n• Damaged flooring hazard — Moderate — 18.2\nInside subtotal: 54.6\nOUTSIDE AREA\n• Trip hazard — Moderate — 16.5\n• Deteriorated walkway — Moderate — 16.5\n• Three Low standards — 2.0 x 3 = 6.0\nOutside subtotal: 39.0\n\nCALCULATE, sample of 8\nUnit: 204.8 / 8 = 25.60\nInside: 54.6 / 8 = 6.83\nOutside: 39.0 / 8 = 4.88\nUNIT THRESHOLD TEST: 25.60 — under 30, but ONLY BY 4.4 POINTS. At a sample of 8, one more Severe unit finding adds 5.00 and would cross it. Flag this prominently.\nTotal deduction: 37.30. PROJECTED SCORE: 62.70.\n\nTHE READ\nA passing score with almost no margin, and a Unit Threshold 4.4 points from triggering. The cause is structural: at a sample of 8, EVERY finding costs eight times what it would at a sample of 64. One Life-Threatening item alone is 7.50 points.\n\nTHE PLAN\n1. Exposed conductor — 24-hour clock, 7.50 points, cost a few dollars. Do it today.\n2. Both entry door deadbolts — 5.00 points, roughly 100 dollars. Note that both must be fixed; one does not remove the deduction.\n3. Sprinkler obstruction — 4.55 points, one hour of labor.\nAfter those three: Unit deduction falls to 12.60, total deduction to 24.80, projected score 75.20. Roughly 150 dollars and half a day.\nThe mold at 3.5 square feet is another 5.00 points but requires finding the moisture source — likely the same absent ventilation driving three other findings. That is one repair scope addressing two standards.\n\nTHE SENTENCE FOR THE CLIENT\n\"You are projected to pass at about 63, and you are four points from an automatic fail on the Unit Threshold. The first 150 dollars of work moves you to about 75 and takes the threshold risk off the table.\""},{"heading":"Scenario 2 — the Unit Threshold trap","body":"THE PROPERTY: 30-unit Public Housing property. Sample of 14. Management reports the site \"looks great\" and the grounds and common areas are genuinely well maintained.\n\nRAW FINDINGS — all in units\n• Two different Life-Threatening findings: blocked egress from a permanently installed window AC in an egress window, and a disconnected water heater flue.\n• Six different Severe findings across the sampled units: broken entry deadbolt; mold-like substance at 2 square feet; water heater TPR discharge terminating 11 inches above the drain; a ground-floor window that cannot be secured; combustibles within three feet of a water heater; and a second mold location at 4 square feet in a different unit under a different standard grouping.\n• Five different Moderate findings: bathroom ventilation absent; inoperable range burner; damaged floor covering hazard; plumbing leak under a kitchen sink; damaged interior door.\nINSIDE and OUTSIDE: nothing recorded. The common areas and site are clean.\n\nCLASSIFY AND CALCULATE\nUnit: 2 LT (120.0) + 6 Severe (240.0) + 5 Moderate (100.0) = 460.0\nUnit deduction: 460.0 / 14 = 32.86\nInside: 0. Outside: 0.\nRaw score: 100 - 32.86 = 67.14.\n\nUNIT THRESHOLD TEST: 32.86 is at or above 30. THE SCORE IS ADJUSTED TO 59. THE PROPERTY FAILS.\n\nTHE READ\nThis is the scenario that blindsides owners, and it is worth pausing on. Every visible indicator is good: clean site, well-kept common areas, no Inside or Outside deductions at all. The raw score of 67 would pass comfortably. The property fails anyway, on in-unit conditions alone, and no amount of additional work outside the units would change it.\n\nTHE PLAN — and note how small it is\nThe threshold needs the Unit deduction below 30, so you must shed more than 2.86 points, which at a sample of 14 is more than 40.1 of weight. That is one Life-Threatening finding, with room to spare.\n• Clear ONE Life-Threatening finding: Unit weight 400.0, deduction 28.57. Threshold cleared. Score 71.43.\n• Clear the second Life-Threatening finding as well: Unit weight 340.0, deduction 24.29. Score 75.71.\n• Add one Severe: 300.0, deduction 21.43. Score 78.57.\n\nBoth Life-Threatening items are on 24-hour clocks anyway. The window AC is removed at no cost. The flue reconnection is a short service call. So the repairs that convert a 59 into a 75.71 are ones the property was already obligated to complete within a day.\n\nWHY MARGIN MATTERS HERE\nDo not stop at 28.57. Your projection is not exact — the actual sample will not be the units you walked, and the inspector may record something you did not. Aim to land the Unit deduction several points under 30, which at a sample of 14 means shedding another Severe or two. Clearing both LT items and one Severe puts you at 21.43, which is genuine margin.\n\nTHE SENTENCE FOR THE CLIENT\n\"Your property is going to fail at 59 with a raw score of 67, entirely because of conditions inside the units. Two repairs you are already required to make within 24 hours take you to about 76.\""},{"heading":"Scenario 3 — the large property just below the line","body":"THE PROPERTY: 220-unit project-based Multifamily property. Sample of 32. Prior score 63. Draft report just issued at 59.49 — a fail by half a point.\n\nTHE DEFICIENCY DETAIL, after de-duplication by standard within area\nUNIT: 2 LT (120.0), 6 Severe (240.0), 14 Moderate (280.0), 9 Low (21.6). Subtotal 661.6. Deduction 20.68\nINSIDE: 1 LT (54.5), 4 Severe (145.6), 9 Moderate (163.8), 7 Low (15.4). Subtotal 379.3. Deduction 11.85\nOUTSIDE: 3 Severe (99.3), 8 Moderate (132.0), 12 Low (24.0). Subtotal 255.3. Deduction 7.98\nTotal deduction 40.51. Score 59.49.\nUNIT THRESHOLD: 20.68, well under 30. Not triggered — this is a genuine Property Threshold failure.\n\nTHE READ\nThe property is 0.51 points from passing. At a sample of 32, 0.51 points is 16.3 of WEIGHT — less than a single Moderate outside deficiency at 16.5.\n\nTHAT CHANGES THE ENTIRE STRATEGY\nBecause this is a DRAFT report, the technical review window is open. The highest-value action is not remediation; it is finding ONE recorded deficiency anywhere on the report that does not meet its standard's written criteria. One successful criteria-not-met filing on any Moderate item converts a failure into a pass.\n\nWHERE TO LOOK, IN ORDER OF YIELD\n1. The 12 Low outside and 9 Low unit items — the most likely to have been recorded below a de minimis floor or without meeting the criteria.\n2. The 8 Moderate outside items — check every trip hazard against 3/4 inch vertical and 2 inches horizontal.\n3. Every sprinkler obstruction against the built-environment and parked-vehicle exclusions.\n4. Every fire extinguisher tag citation against the one-year invoice exclusion.\n5. Any elevator certificate citation at all, which should not have been recorded.\n6. Every mold finding against the 4-square-inch floor and the 1-square-foot and 9-square-foot bands.\n7. Any Unit-area item attributed to a unit not on the sample list.\n\nTHE PARALLEL TRACK\nFile the technical review AND begin remediation, because you cannot rely on the filing succeeding. The remediation ranking here favors Inside: the Inside Life-Threatening item alone is 54.5 of weight, or 1.70 points, which is more than three times what the property needs.\n\nTHE ARITHMETIC WORTH SHOWING THE CLIENT\nClearing just the single Inside Life-Threatening finding takes the score from 59.49 to 61.19 — a pass. One repair. But it is a repair, not a report correction, so it changes the NEXT inspection rather than this one. Only the technical review changes this result.\n\nTHE SENTENCE FOR THE CLIENT\n\"You are half a point from passing, and the technical review window is open for another [n] days. One defensible challenge on any Moderate item converts this to a pass. We should file, and we should also start the remediation, because we cannot count on the filing.\""},{"heading":"Scenario 4 — the failing property and the recovery build","body":"THE PROPERTY: 76-unit Public Housing property. Sample of 20. Final report: 59.74. Failed. No technical review filed and the window has closed. The client wants a plan.\n\nTHE DEFICIENCY DETAIL, after de-duplication\nUNIT: 1 LT (60.0), 4 Severe (160.0), 6 Moderate (120.0), 5 Low (12.0). Subtotal 352.0. Deduction 17.60\nINSIDE: 1 LT (54.5), 3 Severe (109.2), 5 Moderate (91.0), 6 Low (13.2). Subtotal 267.9. Deduction 13.40\nOUTSIDE: 2 Severe (66.2), 6 Moderate (99.0), 10 Low (20.0). Subtotal 185.2. Deduction 9.26\nTotal 40.26. Score 59.74.\nUNIT THRESHOLD: 17.60 — not triggered. Points can be harvested anywhere.\n\nTHE READ\nThis is the pattern where the intuition \"units are weighted most so work the units\" is exactly wrong. Inside and Outside together contributed 22.66 of the 40.26 — more than half the damage — from conditions that are cheap and entirely under management's control.\n\nTHE RECOVERY BUILD, IN THREE PHASES\n\nPHASE 1 — Inside and Outside, no unit entry required\nClear the Inside Life-Threatening item (54.5), all three Inside Severe items (109.2), and both Outside Severe items (66.2). Total weight cleared: 229.9, or 11.50 points.\nNew deductions: Unit 17.60, Inside 5.21, Outside 5.95. Total 28.76. SCORE 71.24.\nTypical cost profile: a panel filler plate, an extinguisher service call, moving stored material away from a boiler and out of a corridor, a door closer, a sidewalk grind. Call it a few hundred dollars and two days of labor.\n\nPHASE 2 — the unit Life-Threatening and Severe items\nClear the Unit LT (60.0) and all four Unit Severe items (160.0). Weight cleared 220.0, or 11.00 points.\nNew deductions: Unit 6.60, Inside 5.21, Outside 5.95. Total 17.76. SCORE 82.24.\nThis phase requires unit entry, resident scheduling and real trade work, and it is where the money is.\n\nPHASE 3 — the tail\nThe remaining Moderate and Low items across all three areas total 355.2 of weight, or 17.76 points. Reaching 90 requires clearing about 8 of those 17.76 points, which is 160 of weight — roughly eight Moderate instances. Whether that is worth doing depends entirely on the cycle argument in Scenario 6.\n\nTHE ORDERING PRINCIPLE\nPhase 1 recovers 11.50 points for a few hundred dollars. Phase 2 recovers 11.00 points for several thousand. Almost identical point yield, an order of magnitude difference in cost. Any plan that starts with Phase 2 is wrong, and every client's instinct is to start with Phase 2 because that is where the visible problems are.\n\nTHE SENTENCE FOR THE CLIENT\n\"Two days of work in your common areas and on your site takes you from a failed 59.7 to a passing 71.2. Everything after that costs real money. Do the cheap eleven points first.\""},{"heading":"Scenario 5 — both thresholds failed, and Scenario 6 — the cycle boundary","body":"SCENARIO 5 — BOTH THRESHOLDS FAILED\nTHE PROPERTY: 48-unit property. Sample of 16.\nUNIT: 3 LT (180.0), 5 Severe (200.0), 6 Moderate (120.0). Subtotal 500.0. Deduction 31.25\nINSIDE: 1 Severe (36.4). Deduction 2.28\nOUTSIDE: 2 Moderate (33.0). Deduction 2.06\nTotal deduction 35.59. Raw score 64.41 — which would PASS.\nUNIT THRESHOLD: 31.25, at or above 30. SCORE ADJUSTED TO 59. FAIL.\n\nTHE READ, AND WHY IT MATTERS THAT BOTH ARE IN PLAY\nStrictly, the raw score of 64.41 passes the Property Threshold and the Unit Threshold is what fails the property. But treat this as a both-thresholds situation, because the raw score's margin is only 4.41 points and any additional finding erodes it. You are near two lines at once.\n\nTHE PLAN, AND THE TRAP\nThe Inside and Outside items here total 69.4 of weight, or 4.34 points. Clearing ALL of them takes the raw score from 64.41 to 68.75 — and the property still fails at 59, because the Unit deduction is unchanged at 31.25.\nThat is the trap. A client who spends their entire budget on the visible, accessible, cheap Inside and Outside work will have improved their raw score by more than four points and changed nothing about the outcome.\nThe only work that matters here is Unit-area work. To get the Unit deduction below 30 you must shed more than 20.0 of weight — exactly one Moderate unit finding, at a sample of 16, is 20.0, which lands you at precisely 30.0 and still triggers. So you need MORE than one Moderate. Clearing one Severe unit finding sheds 40.0, taking the Unit deduction to 28.75 and the score to 62.91.\nClearing one Life-Threatening plus one Severe sheds 100.0, taking the Unit deduction to 25.00 and the score to 68.91 — which is the margin you actually want.\n\nTHE SENTENCE FOR THE CLIENT\n\"Every dollar you spend outside the units is wasted until the unit deductions come down. Two specific in-unit repairs take you from an automatic 59 to about 69.\"\n\nSCENARIO 6 — THE CYCLE BOUNDARY\nTHE PROPERTY: 60-unit Multifamily property. Sample of 20. Comfortably passing.\nUNIT: 2 Severe (80.0), 5 Moderate (100.0), 4 Low (9.6). Subtotal 189.6. Deduction 9.48\nINSIDE: 1 Severe (36.4), 3 Moderate (54.6), 5 Low (11.0). Subtotal 102.0. Deduction 5.10\nOUTSIDE: 4 Moderate (66.0), 8 Low (16.0). Subtotal 82.0. Deduction 4.10\nTotal 18.68. SCORE 81.32. Unit Threshold: 9.48, nowhere near 30.\n\nTHE READ\nThis property passes and nothing here is urgent. The argument is not compliance — it is money, and it turns on the inspection cycle:\n• 90 and above: inspected at least every three years.\n• 80 to 89: every two years.\n• Below 80: annually.\nAt 81.32 this property is on a two-year cycle. Over six years that is three inspection events instead of two.\n\nTHE ARITHMETIC OF REACHING 90\nShed 8.68 points, which at a sample of 20 is 173.6 of weight.\nA workable combination: both Unit Severe items (80.0), the Inside Severe (36.4), two Unit Moderate items (40.0), and one Inside Moderate (18.2). Total 174.6 of weight, or 8.73 points. New score: 90.05.\nThat is six repairs. Not thirty — six, chosen for weight rather than visibility.\n\nRunning it as a full clean-up instead: clearing everything except three Unit Moderate, four Unit Low, two Inside Moderate, five Inside Low, two Outside Moderate and eight Outside Low leaves deductions of 3.48, 2.37 and 2.45 for a total of 8.30 and a score of 91.70. More work, 1.65 points more, and comfortable margin above the boundary.\n\nTHE BUSINESS CASE TO PUT IN WRITING\nEach inspection event costs the client preparation labor, staff disruption, resident notification, corrective work under time pressure, and the risk of a materially worse result. Removing one event from every six-year period is a recurring saving. Quantify it with the client's own numbers rather than asserting it — ask what the last inspection cost them in staff time and emergency repairs, and use that figure.\n\nTHE SENTENCE FOR THE CLIENT\n\"You pass comfortably, so nothing here is urgent. But six specific repairs move you from a two-year cycle to a three-year cycle, which is one fewer inspection every six years. Let us put a number on what an inspection actually costs you and decide whether those six repairs pay for themselves.\""}]}];
export const SCORING_QUIZZES = {"ch14":{"title":"Chapter 14 Quiz — The Defect Impact Weight Table","questions":[{"q":"What is the Defect Impact Weight for a Severe deficiency in the Inside area?","opts":["40.0","36.4","33.1","18.2"],"ans":1,"exp":"Severe: Unit 40.0, Inside 36.4, Outside 33.1."},{"q":"Approximately what multiplier converts a Unit weight to the corresponding Inside and Outside weights?","opts":["0.75 and 0.50","0.91 and 0.83","0.60 and 0.40","0.95 and 0.90"],"ans":1,"exp":"The ratios hold to within a percentage point at every severity level, which makes the table reconstructible in the field from the Unit column alone."},{"q":"One Life-Threatening unit deficiency at a sample of 16 deducts:","opts":["60.0 points","7.50 points","3.75 points","1.88 points"],"ans":2,"exp":"60.0 divided by 16 equals 3.75."},{"q":"Three instances of the SAME Severe standard in three sampled units, sample of 12. Points deducted?","opts":["10.00","3.33","1.11","40.0"],"ans":1,"exp":"The duplicate rule collapses them to one Unit-area instance: 40.0 divided by 12 equals 3.33. Three DIFFERENT standards would be 10.00 — that gap is the largest source of error in amateur projections."},{"q":"In the seven-step projection method, what must you do BEFORE computing the score?","opts":["Rank by cost","Test the Unit Threshold","Photograph everything","Confirm the inspection date"],"ans":1,"exp":"Testing the threshold first prevents you from presenting a comfortable-looking projection that is actually a failure."},{"q":"A 30-unit property, sample 14, has Unit-only findings totalling 460.0 of weight. What is the outcome?","opts":["Score 67.14 — a pass","Unit deduction 32.86 triggers the Unit Threshold; score adjusted to 59 — a fail","Score 59.00 with no adjustment","Score 32.86"],"ans":1,"exp":"460.0 divided by 14 is 32.86, at or above the 30-point limit, so the score is capped at 59 despite a raw 67.14."},{"q":"In that same case, what single action clears the threshold?","opts":["Fixing all the Moderate items","Clearing one Life-Threatening unit finding, which drops the Unit deduction to 28.57 and the score to 71.43","Fixing the Outside items","Nothing can clear it before the inspection"],"ans":1,"exp":"One repair, and the score moves from 59 to 71.43. Being able to say that is the fee."},{"q":"A property fails the Property Threshold at 59.74 with a Unit deduction of 17.60. Where should the budget go?","opts":["Only into Unit-area findings","Wherever points are cheapest, which is usually Inside first — the Unit Threshold is not binding here","Only into Outside findings","Split evenly across the three areas"],"ans":1,"exp":"The two regimes call for genuinely different plans. Choosing the wrong one wastes the entire budget."},{"q":"When the Unit deduction is at or near 30, what does Inside and Outside remediation do for the threshold?","opts":["It reduces it proportionally","Nothing at all — only Unit-area reductions affect the Unit Threshold","It halves it","It defers it"],"ans":1,"exp":"There is no equivalent threshold for Inside or Outside, and their deductions do not enter the Unit test."},{"q":"What is the maximum scoring sample size under NSPIRE, and what was it under UPCS?","opts":["27 now, 32 before","32 now, 27 before","20 now, 32 before","There is no cap"],"ans":1,"exp":"NSPIRE raised the cap to 32. The sample targets 90 percent confidence with a 6 percent margin of error."},{"q":"You have no prior report and cannot confirm the sample size. What is the correct approach?","opts":["Use a published sample-size-by-property-size table as authoritative","Run the projection at two or three plausible sample sizes, present a range, and state the assumption in writing on the front page","Assume the maximum of 32","Decline to project"],"ans":1,"exp":"A projection without a stated sample assumption is a number, not a projection."},{"q":"Which property is EASIER to project accurately?","opts":["One with three isolated bad units among 150","One with a systemic missing-GFCI condition property-wide","They are equally difficult","Neither can be projected"],"ans":1,"exp":"Systemic conditions are deterministic — they will be found and scored. Isolated bad units are probabilistic and require a range."},{"q":"In ranking a punch list, what two things override the points-per-dollar sort?","opts":["Client preference and vendor availability","Anything on a 24-hour clock, and anything needed to clear the Unit Threshold","Cost and season","Area and severity"],"ans":1,"exp":"Safety obligations and threshold-clearing repairs come before ratio optimization."},{"q":"For a repeated deficiency, what does partial remediation recover in score terms?","opts":["A proportional share of the deduction","Nothing — the deduction remains until the condition is eliminated entirely from that area","Half","It depends on the severity"],"ans":1,"exp":"Which is why the cost side of the ratio must be the cost to eliminate EVERY instance, not one."},{"q":"How should deferred-scoring items appear in a ranked punch list?","opts":["Mixed into the main ranking at full value","In a separate block showing zero points recovered before October 1, 2026 and their value after that date","Omitted entirely","At the top of the list"],"ans":1,"exp":"Two totals — today's score and the post-date score — is one of the most useful things you can hand a client right now."}]},"ch15":{"title":"Chapter 15 Quiz — Using the NSPIRE Scoring Calculator","questions":[{"q":"Which of these does the calculator NOT do for you?","opts":["Apply the Defect Impact Weights","Divide by the unit sample","Decide severity classifications and apply the duplicate rule","Subtract from 100"],"ans":2,"exp":"Severity and de-duplication are your inputs. The calculator is precise, not accurate — it will confidently compute a wrong answer from wrong inputs."},{"q":"If you enter five instances of the same standard within the same area, what happens?","opts":["The calculator collapses them automatically","The arithmetic treats them as five — you must de-duplicate before entry","It returns an error","It averages them"],"ans":1,"exp":"The duplicate rule is applied by you, on your own sheet, before anything is entered."},{"q":"Why should de-duplication happen on a dedicated sheet rather than in your head?","opts":["It looks more professional","Because grouping by standard plus area is where the two classic errors occur — collapsing across areas, and collapsing different standards that feel similar","Because HUD requires it","It is faster"],"ans":1,"exp":"Making the grouping explicit and visible is what stops both errors."},{"q":"The sample size should be entered:","opts":["Into each formula individually","Once, in a single clearly labelled cell that every formula references","As a range","Only after the score is computed"],"ans":1,"exp":"One cell means you can re-run the entire projection at a different sample size by changing one number — which is what makes what-if scenarios possible."},{"q":"Your model returns 81 for a sample of 20 with Unit weight 284.8, Inside 72.8, Outside 22.5. Is it working?","opts":["No — the answer should be 75","Yes — deductions of 14.24, 3.64 and 1.13 total 19.01, giving 81","No — the Unit Threshold should trigger","Cannot be determined"],"ans":1,"exp":"This is the reference case from Chapter 4. If your model disagrees, the error is usually in de-duplication or a mis-anchored sample-size reference."},{"q":"Six raw observations — three missing cover plates (two in units, one in a corridor), two missing GFCIs in units, and one undersized guardrail outside — reduce to how many scored instances?","opts":["Six","Four","Three","Two"],"ans":1,"exp":"Cover plates Unit, cover plates Inside, GFCI Unit, and Guardrail Outside — four instances, two of which are deferred from scoring."},{"q":"Why must the cost column reflect eliminating a condition from the entire area?","opts":["For budgeting accuracy only","Because partial remediation of a repeated deficiency recovers no points — only complete elimination removes the deduction","Because HUD audits the cost","It does not matter"],"ans":1,"exp":"A GFCI missing in 71 of 96 units costs 71 receptacles and labor, not one, if the goal is recovering the point."},{"q":"A client asks what happens on October 1, 2026. What should your model produce?","opts":["A single revised score","Both numbers — the score today excluding deferred items, and the score with those items scoring","Only the future score","A qualitative answer"],"ans":1,"exp":"'You are an 84 today and a 79.9 on October 1 unless these six items are closed' is currently the most persuasive sentence available to you."},{"q":"A 60-unit property, sample 20, projects 81.32. How much WEIGHT must be shed to reach 90?","opts":["8.68","173.6","86.8","20"],"ans":1,"exp":"8.68 points times the sample of 20 equals 173.6 of weight. Converting points to weight is what lets you pick specific items from the ranking."},{"q":"When running scenarios, how should each variant be stored?","opts":["By editing the base case","As a labelled column alongside the base case, so all variants can be shown side by side","In a separate file per scenario","Verbally"],"ans":1,"exp":"A model edited into a single optimistic answer is worse than no model, and you will not remember which assumptions changed."},{"q":"Which is the largest source of error in a projection?","opts":["Rounding","Severity misclassification, especially at the Moderate-to-Low boundary where the weights differ by more than eight times","The order of entry","Spreadsheet formulas"],"ans":1,"exp":"The arithmetic is trivial; the classification is where judgment lives and where errors compound."},{"q":"What is the correct way to state a projection in a report?","opts":["A single number with no qualification","A number with a plausible range, the stated sample assumption and its source, the deferred-item comparison, the Unit Threshold status, and an explicit statement that it is not a guarantee","A range only, with no central estimate","A qualitative assessment"],"ans":1,"exp":"Precise about the method, honest about the inputs. Hedging everything makes the report useless; stating assumptions makes it credible."},{"q":"What should a line item say when you cannot verify a criterion against current published guidance?","opts":["Omit the finding","Use your best recollection of the standard","Record the standard name, the location and the measurement, and state that the severity or criterion is to be confirmed against the current standard","Round the severity down"],"ans":2,"exp":"Clients tolerate acknowledged uncertainty. They do not forgive discovered fabrication, and silently dropping a finding is worse than flagging it."},{"q":"Why should you check your model against HUD's own calculator rather than relying on either alone?","opts":["HUD's calculator is always wrong","Yours gives you the intermediate numbers and what-if capability; HUD's confirms the arithmetic against the official implementation","It is required","To satisfy the client"],"ans":1,"exp":"Use HUD's to check your work; use yours to do the work and to answer questions in real time."},{"q":"What must you verify about any calculator before using it on an engagement?","opts":["That it opens correctly","That its weight table and threshold logic match the scoring guidance for the applicable inspection year","That it prints","Nothing"],"ans":1,"exp":"A calculator built on a superseded table produces confident, wrong numbers — worse than no calculator at all."}]},"ch16":{"title":"Chapter 16 Quiz — Reading a REAC Inspection Report","questions":[{"q":"What is the first thing to find in a report you have just been handed?","opts":["The score","The sample size — everything else is arithmetic on that number","The inspector's name","The photographs"],"ans":1,"exp":"The sample size is the divisor for every deduction and the best input you have for future projections."},{"q":"Why does it matter whether the report is DRAFT or FINAL?","opts":["Final reports have more detail","Draft means the technical review window is live; final means it has closed","Draft reports are not official","Draft reports omit the Health and Safety Report"],"ans":1,"exp":"Check which one you are holding before promising a client anything about restoring points."},{"q":"A report shows a score of exactly 59. What should you check?","opts":["Nothing — it is a fail either way","The area breakdown, to see whether this is a genuine 59 or a score adjusted from a higher raw figure by the Unit Threshold","The occupancy information","The certificate images"],"ans":1,"exp":"Those are different situations with different remediation plans. A Unit deduction at or above 30 means an adjusted score."},{"q":"A report shows Unit 31.25, Inside 2.28, Outside 2.06, score 59. What is the correct read?","opts":["A genuine Property Threshold failure","An adjusted score — raw was 64.41 and the Unit deduction of 31.25 crossed the threshold; all remediation money belongs in the Unit area","An arithmetic error on the report","A pass"],"ans":1,"exp":"The Inside and Outside work is worth 4.34 points combined and does nothing for the threshold. A plan that starts with the parking lot fails again."},{"q":"Which area-breakdown pattern is the best news a client can receive?","opts":["Unit-dominated","Inside-dominated — those conditions are cheap, need no resident coordination, and are entirely under management's control","Evenly spread","One enormous finding"],"ans":1,"exp":"Chapter 4's worked example 6 is this pattern: 52.2 to 71.1 without entering a single dwelling unit."},{"q":"A single Life-Threatening item dominates the deduction at a small property. What does that imply?","opts":["Nothing unusual","At a sample of 8 it is 7.50 points on its own, which makes it both the biggest problem and the highest-value technical review target if misclassified","The report is in error","The property automatically fails"],"ans":1,"exp":"Verify the classification carefully whenever one item carries that much of the total."},{"q":"Which of these is a criteria-not-met filing candidate?","opts":["A mold finding recorded at 6 square feet as Severe","A trip hazard recorded where the vertical differential measures 5/8 inch","An exposed conductor at a 2-inch opening","A blocked egress finding for a permanently installed window AC"],"ans":1,"exp":"The vertical criterion is 3/4 inch. The other three all meet their criteria as described."},{"q":"Why is a Unit-area attribution error worth more than its weight suggests?","opts":["Unit weights are double","Because Unit deductions are tested separately against the 30-point Unit Threshold, so reclassification can change a 59 into a real score","Because unit findings cannot otherwise be appealed","It is not worth more"],"ans":1,"exp":"Cross-check every Unit-area line against the units-sampled breakdown."},{"q":"Grouping 31 deficiencies by standard shows 9 ventilation and 11 GFCI findings plus 11 single items. What is the correct read?","opts":["The systemic conditions caused the score problem","The score came mostly from the eleven single items; the money is in the two systemic conditions; and GFCI is deferred and begins deducting October 1, 2026","The report should be appealed","The property will fail"],"ans":1,"exp":"Score problem and cost problem are different problems — and one of the cost problems gets worse on a known date."},{"q":"A 220-unit property, sample 32, scored 59.49 with Unit deduction 20.68. How much weight must be shed to reach 60?","opts":["0.51","16.3 — less than one Moderate outside deficiency","40.51","32"],"ans":1,"exp":"0.51 points times a sample of 32 equals 16.3 of weight. One successful criteria-not-met filing on any Moderate item converts the failure into a pass."},{"q":"Your reconstruction of a score does not match the report. What is the LEAST likely explanation?","opts":["Your de-duplication differs from HUD's","You have a severity wrong","An arithmetic error on the report","You used the wrong sample size"],"ans":2,"exp":"Work through your own de-duplication, severity, area attribution, sample size and weight-table version first. Only after all five are excluded is a report error plausible."},{"q":"Why reconstruct the score even on reports where nothing is in dispute?","opts":["To bill more hours","Because comparing your reconstruction to the reported figure calibrates your severity and de-duplication judgment against HUD's actual application","Because HUD requires it","To check the inspector's credentials"],"ans":1,"exp":"A reconstruction consistently off in one direction reveals a systematic error in your own classification habits."},{"q":"What five numbers should a client see on the first page of your analysis?","opts":["Score, cost, timeline, vendor list, fee","Score and pass/fail; whether the Unit Threshold adjusted it; the three area deductions; the count of 24-hour items and their documentation status; and the technical review deadline","Score, sample size, and three area deductions only","Score alone"],"ans":1,"exp":"Most clients have never seen their own report reduced to five numbers, and doing it establishes immediately that you read it properly."},{"q":"What is the correct posture on technical review grounds?","opts":["Find as many as possible to justify the fee","Most reports contain one or two defensible challenges, not ten — and telling a client when it is not worth filing is what makes you credible when it is","Never file","File only on Life-Threatening findings"],"ans":1,"exp":"A consultant who finds ten grounds on every report is selling, not reading."},{"q":"A client has been focused on their score for three weeks. What should you specifically ask about?","opts":["Their budget","Whether the 24-hour correction obligations from the Health and Safety Report were completed AND documented","Their vendor list","The inspector's name"],"ans":1,"exp":"Correction obligations are independent of the number, and undocumented corrections are a recurring and fixable problem."}]},"ch17":{"title":"Chapter 17 Quiz — Scored Practice Scenarios","questions":[{"q":"In the 8-unit property scenario, what makes it structurally risky?","opts":["The number of findings","At a sample of 8 every finding costs eight times what it would at a sample of 64 — one Life-Threatening item alone is 7.50 points","The property is Public Housing","The Outside findings"],"ans":1,"exp":"Small properties have no statistical cushion. The divisor is the whole story."},{"q":"That 8-unit property projects a Unit deduction of 25.60. Why flag it prominently?","opts":["It exceeds the threshold","It is only 4.4 points from the 30-point Unit Threshold, and at a sample of 8 one more Severe unit finding adds 5.00 and would cross it","It is unusually low","It cannot be reduced"],"ans":1,"exp":"Proximity to the threshold is itself a finding, and margin matters because your projection is not exact."},{"q":"In Scenario 2, the 30-unit property has a Unit deduction of 32.86 and nothing recorded Inside or Outside. What is the score?","opts":["67.14 — a pass","59 — adjusted by the Unit Threshold, a fail","32.86","60 exactly"],"ans":1,"exp":"Raw 67.14, but 32.86 is at or above 30, so the score is capped at 59. Every visible indicator on that property was good."},{"q":"In Scenario 2, what clears the Unit Threshold?","opts":["Clearing all five Moderate items","Clearing one Life-Threatening finding — Unit deduction drops to 28.57 and the score to 71.43","Adding Outside remediation","Nothing before the inspection"],"ans":1,"exp":"More than 40.1 of weight must be shed at a sample of 14; one LT finding is 60.0 with room to spare — and both LT items were on 24-hour clocks anyway."},{"q":"Why should you not stop at a Unit deduction of 28.57 in Scenario 2?","opts":["Because 28.57 still fails","Because the projection is not exact — the actual sample will differ and the inspector may record something you did not, so you want several points of margin","Because HUD rounds up","Because Inside findings will be added"],"ans":1,"exp":"Clearing both LT items and one Severe puts the deduction at 21.43, which is genuine margin."},{"q":"In Scenario 3, the 220-unit property scores 59.49 at a sample of 32. How much weight is needed to reach 60?","opts":["0.51","16.3 — less than one Moderate outside deficiency","51","32"],"ans":1,"exp":"0.51 points times 32 equals 16.3 of weight. That is what makes the technical review the highest-value action."},{"q":"Why is remediation NOT the highest-value action in Scenario 3?","opts":["Remediation is too expensive","Because the report is a DRAFT with the technical review window open — a repair changes the next inspection, only a successful filing changes this result","Because the property already passed","Because the Unit Threshold was triggered"],"ans":1,"exp":"File and remediate in parallel, but understand that only the filing can change this report's score."},{"q":"In Scenario 4, Inside and Outside contributed 22.66 of the 40.26 total deduction. What does that imply?","opts":["The units are fine","More than half the damage came from cheap, management-controlled conditions — so Phase 1 recovers 11.50 points for a few hundred dollars","The report is in error","The Unit Threshold was triggered"],"ans":1,"exp":"This is where the intuition 'units are weighted most so work the units' is exactly wrong."},{"q":"In Scenario 4, Phase 1 recovers 11.50 points and Phase 2 recovers 11.00. What is the difference?","opts":["Nothing meaningful","Cost — Phase 1 is a few hundred dollars and two days; Phase 2 is several thousand and requires unit entry and resident scheduling","Phase 2 is faster","Phase 1 does not affect the score"],"ans":1,"exp":"Near-identical point yield, order-of-magnitude cost difference. Any plan that starts with Phase 2 is wrong."},{"q":"In Scenario 5, clearing ALL Inside and Outside findings improves the raw score by 4.34 points. What happens to the outcome?","opts":["The property passes","Nothing — the Unit deduction is unchanged at 31.25, so the score is still adjusted to 59","The Unit Threshold clears","The score drops"],"ans":1,"exp":"This is the trap: an entire budget spent on visible, accessible work that changes nothing about the result."},{"q":"In Scenario 5, at a sample of 16, clearing exactly one Moderate unit finding takes the Unit deduction to what — and is that enough?","opts":["30.0 exactly, which still triggers the threshold","28.75, which clears it","25.0, which clears it comfortably","31.25, unchanged"],"ans":0,"exp":"20.0 of weight divided by 16 is 1.25 points, taking 31.25 to exactly 30.0 — still at or above the limit. You need more than one Moderate."},{"q":"In Scenario 6, the property scores 81.32. What is the business argument for further work?","opts":["Compliance risk","The inspection cycle — at 81.32 it is on a two-year cycle; at 90 it moves to a three-year cycle, which is one fewer inspection event every six years","The Unit Threshold","Resident satisfaction"],"ans":1,"exp":"Nothing here is urgent, so the argument has to be money rather than compliance."},{"q":"In Scenario 6, how much WEIGHT must be shed at a sample of 20 to move from 81.32 to 90?","opts":["8.68","173.6","86.8","20"],"ans":1,"exp":"8.68 points times a sample of 20 equals 173.6 of weight — achievable with six specific repairs chosen for weight rather than visibility."},{"q":"Across all six scenarios, what single test should be run BEFORE computing the score?","opts":["The cost ranking","The Unit Threshold test","The sample-size sensitivity","The deferred-item block"],"ans":1,"exp":"Running it first prevents you from presenting a comfortable-looking projection that is actually a failure."},{"q":"What caveat should accompany every one of these projections in a real engagement?","opts":["That the client must sign a waiver","That the figures apply the published weight table at a stated sample size, and both the current table and the property's actual sample size must be confirmed before relying on the method","That HUD may change the passing score","That inspections are random"],"ans":1,"exp":"State the method, date the table, disclose the sample assumption. That is what makes the projection defensible."}]}};
export const SCORING_UNIT_TEST = {"u6":{"title":"Unit 6 Cumulative Test — Scoring Deep Dive","passingScore":75,"questions":[{"q":"The Defect Impact Weight for a Severe deficiency Inside is:","opts":["40.0","36.4","33.1","18.2"],"ans":1,"exp":"Severe: Unit 40.0, Inside 36.4, Outside 33.1."},{"q":"Approximately what multipliers convert a Unit weight to Inside and Outside?","opts":["0.75 and 0.50","0.91 and 0.83","0.60 and 0.40","0.95 and 0.90"],"ans":1,"exp":"The ratios hold within a percentage point across every severity row."},{"q":"Three instances of the SAME Severe standard in three sampled units, sample of 12. Points deducted?","opts":["10.00","3.33","1.11","40.0"],"ans":1,"exp":"One Unit-area instance: 40.0 divided by 12. Three DIFFERENT standards would be 10.00."},{"q":"What must be done before computing the score?","opts":["Rank by cost","Test the Unit Threshold","Confirm the inspection date","Photograph everything"],"ans":1,"exp":"Testing first prevents presenting a comfortable-looking projection that is actually a failure."},{"q":"A 30-unit property, sample 14, has Unit-only findings totalling 460.0 of weight. Outcome?","opts":["Score 67.14 — a pass","Unit deduction 32.86 triggers the threshold; score adjusted to 59 — a fail","Score 59 with no adjustment","Score 32.86"],"ans":1,"exp":"The raw score of 67.14 is irrelevant once the Unit deduction reaches 30."},{"q":"In that case, what single action clears the threshold?","opts":["Fixing all Moderate items","Clearing one Life-Threatening unit finding — deduction falls to 28.57 and the score to 71.43","Fixing Outside items","Nothing"],"ans":1,"exp":"One repair, 59 to 71.43. Being able to say that is the fee."},{"q":"A property fails at 59.74 with a Unit deduction of 17.60. Where does the budget go?","opts":["Only into Unit findings","Wherever points are cheapest, usually Inside first — the Unit Threshold is not binding","Only Outside","Split evenly"],"ans":1,"exp":"The two regimes call for genuinely different plans."},{"q":"A 220-unit property, sample 32, scores 59.49. How much weight is needed to reach 60?","opts":["0.51","16.3 — less than one Moderate outside deficiency","51","32"],"ans":1,"exp":"Which makes a single criteria-not-met technical review filing the highest-value action available."},{"q":"In a report, what should you locate first?","opts":["The score","The sample size","The inspector's name","The photographs"],"ans":1,"exp":"Everything else is arithmetic on that number, and it is the best input for future projections."},{"q":"A report shows Unit 31.25, Inside 2.28, Outside 2.06 and a score of 59. Correct read?","opts":["A genuine Property Threshold failure","An adjusted score — raw was 64.41 and the Unit deduction crossed the threshold; all money belongs in the Unit area","An arithmetic error","A pass"],"ans":1,"exp":"The Inside and Outside work is worth 4.34 points and does nothing for the threshold."},{"q":"Your score reconstruction does not match the report. What is the LEAST likely explanation?","opts":["Your de-duplication differs from HUD's","You have a severity wrong","An arithmetic error on the report","You used the wrong sample size"],"ans":2,"exp":"Work through your own de-duplication, severity, area attribution, sample size and weight-table version first."},{"q":"What two things override a points-per-dollar ranking?","opts":["Client preference and vendor availability","Anything on a 24-hour clock, and anything needed to clear the Unit Threshold","Cost and season","Area and severity"],"ans":1,"exp":"Safety obligations and threshold-clearing repairs come before ratio optimization."},{"q":"How should deferred-scoring items appear in a projection?","opts":["Mixed in at full value","In a separate block, with the score shown both excluding and including them","Omitted","At the top of the ranking"],"ans":1,"exp":"Two totals — today's score and the post-October-2026 score — is the most useful comparison you can give a scored-program owner right now."},{"q":"How should a projection be stated in a report?","opts":["A single number","A number with a plausible range, the stated sample assumption and its source, the deferred-item comparison, the Unit Threshold status, and an explicit statement that it is not a guarantee","A range with no central estimate","Qualitatively"],"ans":1,"exp":"Precise about the method, honest about the inputs."},{"q":"What should a line item say when a criterion cannot be verified against current published guidance?","opts":["Omit the finding","Use your best recollection","Record the standard, location and measurement, and state that the criterion or severity is to be confirmed against the current standard","Round the severity down"],"ans":2,"exp":"Clients tolerate acknowledged uncertainty; they do not forgive discovered fabrication."}]}};
